Florence model bundle

Origin, permission and citation

The Florence model is the NMRD program for paramagnetic enhancements of
nuclear relaxation rates in slowly rotating systems by I. Bertini, O. Galas,
C. Luchinat and G. Parigi, distributed by CERM, University of Florence:
https://www.cerm.unifi.it/research/software/19-research/164-nmrd

Giacomo Parigi and Adam Kubrak confirmed in writing (email of 2026-10-08)
that "the Florence/NMRD code may be redistributed and modified as part of
OneFit-Engine under the Artistic License 2.0, with attribution to the
original authors and citation of J Magn Reson Ser A 113:151 (1995), for the
original Florence model, and J Chem Phys 111:5795 (1999) and Phys Chem
3:4907 (2001) for the modified Florence model."

Work that uses these functions must cite:

- the original Florence model (FLAG < 2 or FLAG >= 3):
  I. Bertini, O. Galas, C. Luchinat, G. Parigi,
  J. Magn. Reson. Ser. A 113, 151 (1995);
- the modified Florence model (2 <= FLAG < 3, Florence_f.f's MODFLOR):
  I. Bertini, J. Kowalewski, C. Luchinat, T. Nilsson, G. Parigi,
  J. Chem. Phys. 111, 5795 (1999), and
  D. Kruk, T. Nilsson, J. Kowalewski,
  Phys. Chem. Chem. Phys. 3, 4907 (2001).

The Florence C and Fortran sources previously included externally sourced
numerical routines whose redistribution terms required review before this
bundle could be published or included in a binary release.

Update: the NAG-derived complex Hermitian eigensolver (F01BCF, the original
body of F02AXF, F02AYF, P01ABF, and P01ABZ) has been replaced, preserving
F02AXF's exact call signature, IFAIL semantics, and eigenvector
phase-normalization convention so no caller needed any change. No NAG
copyright notices or NAG Library routine names remain in Florence_f.f as of
this change.

Correction (florence 0.3.1, 2026-10-30): the statement above was not
true until 0.3.1. An audit found that releases 0.1.0 to 0.3.0 still
contained, from NAG's F02AXF, its eigenvector normalisation statements
(the same statements, with renumbered labels) and its routine name
F02AXF, plus an error handler and message helpers equivalent to NAG's
P01ABF, P01ABZ, X04AAF and X04BAF under other names (OFP01ABF,
OFP01ABZ, OFERRUNIT, OFERRMSG). Florence 0.3.1 removes all of them:
the eigensolver entry point is now OFHEIG, written from its
specification only (eigenvalues ascending; unit-length eigenvectors
whose first largest-modulus component is real and positive; IFAIL 0 =
success, 1 = no convergence, 2 = non-real diagonal; with IFAIL = 0 on
entry a failure prints a message and stops). Every Florence output in
the golden regression test is bit-identical before and after.
A full audit then compared Florence_f.f with every NAG routine of the
original file (F01BCF, F02AXF, F02AYF and the eight helpers), both
statement by statement and with all names and numbers masked, so that
renamed copies would show. Two loops of the Householder + QL solver
had the same statement structure as NAG's F02AYF - the plane rotation
of the eigenvector columns (9 statements) and the eigenvalue sort
(8 statements) - and were rewritten in 0.3.1 (a rotation through
temporaries; a sort that records a permutation and then gathers the
columns). Outputs are bit-identical on a grid of 10,800 Florence
values. What remains in common are generic runs of at most 5
statements (copying or zeroing an array, a swap through a temporary)
and two single lines (a RETURN for N = 1, the QL loop test M = L). Such
patterns go back to the published tql2 procedure of Wilkinson and
Reinsch's Handbook for Automatic Computation (1971), which NAG's,
EISPACK's and CERN MINUIT's eigensolvers all follow. This is a
textual comparison, not a legal review.

Florence 0.3.3 (2026-10-30) has the same code as 0.3.2. Its repository
history starts at 0.3.3: the earlier history, which contains the
versions with NAG-derived code, was moved to a private archive and is
not redistributed.

Florence 0.3.5 (2026-10-30): OFHEIG's failure stop exits with status 1
(it exited with 0, so a fit stopped by it looked successful), and OFHEIG
scales the matrix by a power of 2 that brings its largest element near
1 before solving, then scales the eigenvalues back - exact, so Florence
outputs are bit-identical, but matrices from 1e-300 to 1e300 now solve
(the sums of squares in CHTRIDR underflowed below about 1e-150 and
overflowed above about 1e150). A NaN or infinite element is IFAIL 3.

This went through two implementations. The first replaced the NAG code with
LAPACK's ZHEEV (BSD-3-Clause), verified numerically equivalent via (1) a
synthetic head-to-head comparison against the original NAG-derived code
across random complex Hermitian matrices of several sizes (max eigenvalue
difference 7.5e-14, max eigenvector difference 9.7e-15 - floating-point
round-off noise, not a discrepancy), and (2) this bundle's own
golden-output regression test (models/florence/tests/run_tests.sh),
re-run against the real Florence physics model end to end (max relative
difference 6.4e-16 - single-ULP noise). That version was measured live to
make real Florence fits 1.5-2x slower than the original NAG code: this
routine runs once per MINUIT chi2 evaluation on matrices that are tiny in
practice (N=6-8 for the models this was measured against), where a
general-purpose LAPACK routine's per-call overhead dominates over the
arithmetic itself - not fixable by workspace tuning, which was tried and
made no difference.

A second attempt replaced ZHEEV with a hand-rolled cyclic-Jacobi
eigensolver (CHEEVJ/CJROT), removing LAPACK's per-call overhead entirely.
It was correct (verified against known-answer cases and ZHEEV directly
across hundreds of random Hermitian matrices) but, measured live, still
slower than ZHEEV - Jacobi's method does asymptotically more arithmetic
per solve than Householder+QL (several full O(N^2) sweeps versus one
O(N^3) reduction plus a cheaper iteration), so removing LAPACK's overhead
wasn't enough to also beat LAPACK's algorithm choice.

A third attempt (CHEEVHQ/CHTRID/TRIDQL_Z, since removed/superseded) backed
F02AXF with a hand-rolled Householder-tridiagonalization + implicit-QL
eigensolver using native COMPLEX*16 arithmetic - the same algorithm family
the original NAG code and LAPACK's ZHEEV both use internally, written from
the mathematical description (standard, decades-old published numerical
linear algebra, e.g. Golub & Van Loan's "Matrix Computations"), not
derived from NAG's, LAPACK's, or EISPACK's source, and verified in three
stages (CHTRID alone, TRIDQL_Z alone, then the combined pipeline - known-
answer cases plus a random-Hermitian-matrix sweep vs ZHEEV, N=2..30). An
isolated per-call microbenchmark suggested this should beat ZHEEV by
~1.9x and the original NAG code outright, but measured live on a real
Florence fit it was only ~1.7x slower than NAG (real-world embedded
performance did not match the isolated benchmark - most likely cache-
locality effects: a tight microbenchmark loop keeps everything hot in L1
cache in a way a real fit's much larger surrounding computation does not).
This was real, measured progress (down from ZHEEV's ~2x and Jacobi's
~2.7x slowdown) but not a full win.

Analyzing (not copying) the original NAG code's source found it never
used native COMPLEX*16 arithmetic at all - both F01BCF and F02AYF kept
real and imaginary parts as separate DOUBLE PRECISION arrays throughout,
a well-known, generic 1970s-80s Fortran performance idiom (also used in
EISPACK/LINPACK) for avoiding compiler overhead in native complex
arithmetic. F02AXF is now backed by CHEEVHQR/CHTRIDR/TRIDQLR, which apply
that same general technique to the CHEEVHQ version's own already-derived
formulas (every complex multiply/add hand-expanded into its real and
imaginary components) - not a line of NAG's code, just the same
structural idea, independently re-derived. AR/AI are used directly with
no complex-packing step. Verified the same way as CHEEVHQ (known-answer
cases plus the random-Hermitian-matrix sweep vs ZHEEV, N=2..30) before
being wired in - see models/florence/tests/test_cheevhqr.f. Golden-output
regression re-run against the real physics model: byte-identical to the
COMPLEX*16 version's output (same algorithm, same operation order).
Measured live on the same real Florence fit: ~1.63x slower than the
original NAG code (33.0s vs 20.2s), a small further improvement over the
COMPLEX*16 version's ~1.69x (34.1s) - consistent with the isolated
microbenchmark's finding of no meaningful per-call difference between the
two arithmetic styles for this workload. Full real-fit comparison across
every version attempted (all on the same fit, florenceN4LS_fH_R1OSabh_test.json,
N=8, 5 trials each): NAG 20.2s (baseline), ZHEEV 40.6s (+101%), Jacobi
54.8s (+171%), Householder+QL/COMPLEX*16 34.1s (+69%), Householder+QL/
split-real 33.0s (+63%). Chi2 differs by ~6% across every Householder+QL
attempt (1.53 vs 1.45) - consistent with ordinary MINUIT-iterative-
optimizer path sensitivity to which floating-point algorithm computes the
model function, not a correctness problem (each algorithm's own golden-
output regression against itself passes to ULP-level precision).

Two further performance angles were investigated and found not
applicable: (1) TRIDQL's QL-iteration count on real Florence matrices is
normal (avg ~1.8 iterations per eigenvalue, max 5) - not the cause of the
gap to NAG; (2) F02AXF's input matrix changes on every single call within
a fit (measured repeat fraction: exactly zero) - the underlying physics
requires diagonalizing a different matrix at every data point, so no
form of result caching applies here.

Given four real implementations attempted and no further concrete,
promising lead identified, this is considered the final state pending
further external investigation.

